22. Key Findings

Finding 1 -- Market populations are highly uneven

Global and US have significantly larger populations than many other
markets, while multiple markets contain only a handful of policies.

Conclusion: policy count alone cannot be assumed to represent
complete market coverage.

Finding 2 -- objRel coverage is inconsistent

Some markets have relationships while others have none.

Conclusion: absence of objRel should not automatically be
interpreted as missing data.

Finding 3 -- REG relationships are concentrated in US data

Regulatory relationships were observed in the US output but not in the
other examined markets or Global.

Conclusion: this requires confirmation of the regulatory
relationship rollout/source.

Finding 4 -- Global and market-level relationship models differ

Global appears to use sgmntTypeCde more directly, while market data
uses objRel for CNTRL relationships.

Conclusion: the relationship between these two mechanisms must be
established before combining them.

Finding 5 -- PLCY relationships require business interpretation

The presence of policy-to-policy relationships indicates that policies
can be linked to other policies.

Conclusion: the exact semantics and directionality of these
relationships must be confirmed.

Finding 6 -- Segment information is consistently populated

The visible EDA results show 100% population of sgmntTypeCde for the
displayed markets.

Conclusion: segment information appears substantially more complete
than objRel coverage.

Finding 7 -- Text completeness varies significantly

Some markets show extremely short policy text.

Conclusion: text fields should not be used for NLP or semantic
analysis until the authoritative content source is confirmed.

